  Summary: Concurrent POST /checkout requests reserve inventory by running one UPDATE per cart item, in the order the client sent the items, with no sorting. Two transactions that touch the same SKUs in opposite order deadlock, Postgres aborts one, and checkout returns 500. This produced about 124 deadlock errors in 15 minutes and pushed the 5xx ratio to about 5-6%, above the 5% SLO.
   - shop_api logged 124 'deadlock detected' errors in reserve_inventory (inventory.py:7) in the 15-minute window, starting about 1.8 minutes before the alert.  [s2, s6]
   - The 5xx ratio rose from about 0.4% to about 5-6% and crossed the 5% alert threshold at about 06:12-06:13.  [s3]
   - db_locks shows the blocking chain is entirely shop_api sessions (pids 62524, 62525, 62526, 62527, 62576) all running the same inventory UPDATE, with 8 deadlocks in the last 15 minutes.  [s4]
   - reserve_inventory issues one UPDATE per item in the caller's item order with no sort by SKU, so concurrent carts that share SKUs in different orders acquire row locks in opposite order.  [s5, s7]
   - No deploys or config changes were recorded in the last 3 hours, so the trigger is the existing code under the current traffic mix.  [s8]
   - The failing POST /checkout trace returned 500 after about 1 second, which fits a transaction aborted after lock waits.  [s9]
  Fix: In services/shop_api/app/inventory.py, sort items by sku before the loop in reserve_inventory so every transaction locks rows in the same global order. Optionally add a bounded retry for deadlock errors (SQLSTATE 40P01) at the checkout transaction level. Also investigate the separate NoResultFound in receipts.py:11 render_receipt (30 errors), which is unrelated to the deadlock.

